Z||000000||GT Bastard||08-07-2000||04:18 PM||||Lunati will apparently make a custom grind cam for th 60 degree V6.  They also advertise roller cams, so I'm guessing they'd be able to produce a roller cam for us as well(price isn't bad at all.)  Excuse my ignorance, but what is required to run a roller cam in a 60 degree V6? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shaun41178||08-07-2000||08:35 PM||||It is my understanding that a roller cam and roller lifters can not be used with our 2.8-2.4 pushrod engines when we use the lower fiero intake plenum.  I have been told that the lifters when being pushed up by the cam will come into contact with the lower plenum.  i have had my engine apart and it looks like this is a definate possibilty although I have yet to try a roller cam to see for sure.  If lunati can make a cam and some sort of a low profile lifter to keep from hitting the intake then I would buy one.  <P>Rollers produce much more power then non-roller as with rollers the ramps can be much steeper allowing the valve to be at max lift for a longer time without increasing overall valve duration.<P>Keep me informed please.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||08-08-2000||03:12 AM||||I'm going to see what I can find out from them...||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocrazy||08-08-2000||08:40 AM||||well i will try to shed some light on this.  I dont beleive they ever made a 2.8 roller although I know they made a roller block for a 3.1 , not sure which one (maybe 92 beretta GT)i beleive you can use the 2.8 steel heads and fiero intake, I am not sure about the clearance with the steel heads but the 3.1 is ok but then you would have to use the 3.1 intake but you would have a roller.  I think ||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||08-08-2000||10:36 PM||||FWIW, I do know Crane makes cams with a small base circle for a roller conversion kit for non-roller ford blocks because the lifter bores aren't high enough to retain the lifters.  That might solve the lifter going too high situation, but you still have the problem of keeping the lifters from spinning in the bores.  One way to remedy that is to buy some lifter that are attached to each other in pairs by a bar (pretty darn expensive~$4-500), or rig up some sort of retainer that holds the lifters in their bores without spinning.  <P>Personally, I wouldn't try an rig anything a cam company doesn't offer as a kit, but thats just me.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">  <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
